You misunderstand.  Right now it's possible to accurately calculate
exactly how many items per square is 'perfectly safe' to polymorph (i
think it works out to 4 per square and blessed, or something.)

I want to change that number to 1 per square.  In other words, you can
safely poly one item per square with no risk of golem generation or
other ill effects beyond vanilla's current (which i believe is 'none').

> nerf.  Perhaps cutting the reward/risk ratio in half by either
> doubling the chance of golem creation or doubling the chance of
> shuddering might be more appropriate.

...And this is what I'm considering doing if you have _more_ than one
item per square -- ie. you now will always have a chance of golem
creation instead of successful poly, and possibly some other creative
risks (I don't like the idea of just making the items vanish any more
often; I'm sure there's something else that can be done to increase
risks in a non-golem fashion.)

Polypiling can stay high-reward, I just want to be sure that risks get
bolted on.  (This way the Wizard can still poly his starting spellbooks 
safely at one per square, for example, but if you go stackin' up rings
or scrolls, you're going to have to figure out a different approach.)

